1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,900
Every scientific paper makes promises.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,320
Beetle larvae leave the ground at

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:07,180
almost half a metre per second.

4
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:09,880
Male peacock spiders take off at

5
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:10,960
twenty-five degrees.

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,520
Often the journal also publishes

7
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:15,220
the data behind them —

8
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,760
the tables, even the videos.

9
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:19,700
But does anyone check one against the other?

10
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:21,740
Almost never.

11
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,860
So we built an agent that does.

12
00:00:24,998 --> 00:00:26,518
You give it exactly what the

13
00:00:26,538 --> 00:00:27,958
journal published.

14
00:00:27,978 --> 00:00:28,558
Nothing more.

15
00:00:29,478 --> 00:00:31,498
Muse Spark reads it all and drafts a plan:

16
00:00:32,438 --> 00:00:34,218
what each file and column means,

17
00:00:34,238 --> 00:00:36,638
and every number the paper claims.

18
00:00:37,358 --> 00:00:39,378
Then code checks every single

19
00:00:39,398 --> 00:00:40,978
proposal against the files.

20
00:00:41,578 --> 00:00:44,798
The rule is simple: the model may propose —

21
00:00:44,818 --> 00:00:47,018
but no number in a verdict is ever

22
00:00:47,038 --> 00:00:48,498
written by a language model.

23
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:52,013
Every step lands in a ledger —

24
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:53,033
what the model proposed,

25
00:00:53,053 --> 00:00:56,433
and the check that accepted or rejected it.

26
00:00:56,453 --> 00:00:58,133
A quote that isn't really in the paper?

27
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:01,333
Rejected — and Muse Spark is asked again,

28
00:01:01,353 --> 00:01:02,313
with the reason.

29
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:05,099
Take the peacock spider paper.

30
00:01:05,799 --> 00:01:07,599
Its take-off angles only add up

31
00:01:07,619 --> 00:01:09,299
as circular means —

32
00:01:09,319 --> 00:01:10,939
exactly the statistics the

33
00:01:10,959 --> 00:01:12,799
authors said they used.

34
00:01:12,819 --> 00:01:14,939
Three of its printed values turn out to be

35
00:01:14,959 --> 00:01:17,119
the table's numbers cut short,

36
00:01:17,139 --> 00:01:18,219
not rounded.

37
00:01:18,239 --> 00:01:22,099
Across thirteen papers — athletes, fish, ants,

38
00:01:22,759 --> 00:01:23,819
birds, spiders.

39
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:26,999
Three hundred and nineteen printed numbers.

40
00:01:28,899 --> 00:01:30,719
Sixty-three reproduce to the printed digit.

41
00:01:30,739 --> 00:01:33,159
For every other one, it tells you why not.

42
00:01:34,258 --> 00:01:35,578
When there's a video,

43
00:01:35,598 --> 00:01:37,378
it goes all the way down to physics.

44
00:01:38,158 --> 00:01:39,358
In the beetle paper,

45
00:01:39,378 --> 00:01:42,578
it rejects eleven of twelve shots up front —

46
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:44,778
no scale, or a camera too slow.

47
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:46,618
One jump is left.

48
00:01:47,538 --> 00:01:49,218
SAM 3.1 tracks it,

49
00:01:49,238 --> 00:01:51,378
the agent finds its row in the data blind,

50
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:53,718
from the jump's shape alone —

51
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:56,378
and the published take-off speed holds up.

52
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:56,678
Reproduced.

53
00:01:58,024 --> 00:01:59,984
The spider video is trickier.

54
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:02,284
The agent splits recordings that

55
00:02:02,304 --> 00:02:04,104
fade into each other,

56
00:02:04,124 --> 00:02:06,844
reads "playback four times faster",

57
00:02:06,864 --> 00:02:09,064
and measures the gap to the target.

58
00:02:09,084 --> 00:02:10,364
Then it refuses.

59
00:02:11,124 --> 00:02:13,104
The flight isn't a clean arc —

60
00:02:13,744 --> 00:02:15,904
something slows the spider down,

61
00:02:15,924 --> 00:02:18,524
almost straight against its motion —

62
00:02:18,544 --> 00:02:21,344
the mark of air drag on a tiny body.

63
00:02:21,364 --> 00:02:23,864
An honest "I can't measure this" beats

64
00:02:23,884 --> 00:02:25,304
a confident wrong number.

65
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:27,060
We tried.

66
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:29,220
Twelve kinds of deliberately

67
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:30,780
wrong model answers.

68
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:32,640
Four were caught,

69
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:35,140
six the agent repaired on its own,

70
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:36,720
two changed nothing —

71
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:38,940
and not one produced a wrong verdict.

72
00:02:40,931 --> 00:02:41,411
Muse Spark proposes.

73
00:02:42,371 --> 00:02:43,751
SAM 3.1 tracks.

74
00:02:44,611 --> 00:02:46,971
Code checks every step, and writes it down.

75
00:02:47,611 --> 00:02:48,431
Pixels to Proof —

76
00:02:49,071 --> 00:02:51,131
a second reader for the numbers in science.

